
# ============================================================================
# Merlin ontology - top-level index. The `using` lines below pull in the
# kind taxonomy + relation taxonomy that make up the entire ontology.
#
# Design framework - read before adding kinds / states / tasks. The rest
# of this file is just `using` lines; the rules live here so any agent
# editing the ontology sees them first.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Acts: tasks and ACTIONs (and nothing else)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every "act" - everything a human does that the system models - is one of:
#
#   * a TASK (declared in Tasks.mon under `act/task`). A task is a mini-
#     plan that bubbles down to an ACTION sequence at isim time and to its
#     hsim event's effect verb at hsim time. Tasks may carry motors
#     (the typical motor mix they need), construed_act tags (how appraisal
#     categorises them), theme tags (which semantic-summary they feed),
#     and may be referenced as goal-clause inner labels.
#
#   * an ACTION (declared in interactive/actions/*.act). An ACTION is the
#     lowest-level motor primitive - WALK_TO, GRASP, SAY, INGEST, POUR.
#     ACTIONs directly affect the shared Merlin env; tasks do not. ACTIONs
#     auto-register as kinds under `act` from the `.act` files; the
#     construed_act analogue for ACTIONs is the `nature = <tag>` line in
#     the `.act` file.
#
# Goal / task / state composition. A `goal` belief targets a TASK
# (`{actor goal {actor kill victim}}` = wants to do the kill task), a
# STATE (`{actor goal {victim condition dead}}` = wants victim dead),
# or a sub-task-or-state pattern via meta-tasks like `urge`
# (`{mother urge {son avoid {son marry girl}}}`). These compose - they
# do not compete. An NPC may hold an ongoing-task belief AND a goal
# targeting the same task simultaneously: both wants to AND is doing it.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Abstract goal-tasks vs leaf method-tasks
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tasks come in two grain sizes:
#
#   * Abstract goal-tasks (kill / coerce / steal / expose / hurt / punish
#     / humiliate / discredit / bribe / frame / seduce / silence_witness)
#     are what deliberation mints as goal-clause inner labels. They name
#     a CLASS of outcome - "actor wants this victim dead" - without
#     committing to method. Abstract goal-tasks carry NO method aux.
#
#   * Leaf method-tasks (strangle / shoot / poison / spread_rumour /
#     plant_evidence / etc.) are the specific tasks an actor performs to
#     satisfy a goal-task. The perpetration selector picks a leaf task
#     from those whose `:requires` substrate predicates match. The
#     anchor minted at task completion is the leaf-task belief itself
#     (`{actor strangle victim}`), NOT a goal-task belief with a method
#     aux. The crime ledger records both axes independently: goal_label
#     names what the actor wanted to achieve, task names what they
#     actually did.
#
# Means (weapon kind, substance kind, blade) are NOT task fields. They
# live in the substrate: the grip edge (spatial ?item gripped_by) names what's in the
# actor's hand at the act timestamp; prior `add_substance` beliefs
# record what was added to which vessel. Walk those at appraisal time
# to recover the means; do not duplicate them on the leaf-task anchor.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Construals are tags, not beliefs
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The appraisal cascade categorises a task by reading the `construed_act`
# tags on the task's declaration (e.g.
# `frame ... (construed_act harm_act betray_act wrong_act)`) at the anchor's
# task-label. It does NOT mint construal beliefs. Anchors carry their tags
# via the relation_def lookup; categorize runs one reaction row per tag
# per POV (patient / actor / third_party / sadist). Promote a construal
# to a belief only if RETE alpha-indexing genuinely requires it.
#
# Outcome is a substrate property, not a tag bucket. A failed kill is a
# leaf kill-task whose victim survived; the anchor and its tags are the
# same whether the victim died or lived. Survivor cascades differ
# because more living perceivers run the standard reaction rows, not
# because the anchor's tags changed.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A noun is automatically a state
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every noun declared in Concepts.mon / Objects.mon / Spaces.mon / etc.
# is automatically usable as a state, and any env attr with that name
# contributes the physical realisation. States.mon holds only the
# verb / predicate states (know, owe, like, hate, avoid, ...) that are
# NOT nouns.
#
# Avoid is a state, not a task. `{son avoid {son marry girl}}` reads
# "son is in the avoid-state of the marry-girl task". Composes with
# meta-tasks (`urge` targets an avoid-state) and with goal beliefs
# (a goal can target an avoid-state). The silenced-witness state is
# `{witness avoid {witness tell secret}}`.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plural-kind attrs: blemish unifies wound / stain / mark
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Forensic traces (body wounds, surface stains, surface marks) share
# the `(type kind array 4) (per obs) (auto-percept)` shape. The
# `blemish` parent kind in Objects.mon unifies them so the `blemishes`
# attr (common.arc) can hold any mix; new writers should target
# `blemishes` directly.
#
# ============================================================================

# Concepts, names, objects, etc.

using "Concepts"
using "EnglishNames"
using "IrishNames"
using "StRevierNames"
using "Objects"
using "Smells"
using "Sounds"
using "Spaces"
using "Locations"

# Relations

using "States"
using "Tasks"

